<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=beekeeping">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beekeeping</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3100 BCE</span> · Also known as apiculture</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beekeeping, or apiculture, is the maintenance of bee colonies, commonly in artificial beehives, to collect honey and other products such as beeswax and propolis. The earliest evidence of humans collecting honey comes from Spanish cave paintings dated to 6,000 BCE, but evidence of beekeeping being practised appears only from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 3,100 BCE. Workers are shown blowing smoke into hives on the walls of the sun temple of Nyuserre Ini, from before 2,422 BCE. The oldest archaeological finds directly relating to beekeeping are thirty intact hives of straw and unbaked clay found at Rehov, Israel, dating from about 900 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beekeeping.jpg" alt="Beekeeping" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: COD Newsroom · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beekeeper_2017_Honeybee_Conservancy,_College_of_DuPage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economy rested on millet and other grains, orchards, <b>beekeeping</b> and above all livestock, and Circassian horses, such as the Kabardian breed, were famous.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Peasants grew rye, oats and barley on the poor soils of the forest zone, supplementing farming with fishing, hunting, <b>beekeeping</b> and the trapping of furs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They lived in small villages and hill forts and farmed the drier ground, supplementing this with hunting, fishing and <b>beekeeping</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dregovichs">Dregovichs</a></li><li><blockquote>Like its neighbours, it lived from maize farming, <b>beekeeping</b> and cotton weaving.</blockquote><a href="/state/hocaba">Hocabá</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3100 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Egypt</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Beekeeping in pottery vessels is also reported beginning about 9,000 years ago in Anatolia, beeswax traces in Middle East potsherds from about 7,000 BCE, and depictions of honey collecting about 10,000 years ago; 3,100 BCE is given as the first evidence from Egypt of beekeeping being practised.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/domestication">Domestication of animals<span>by 13,000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/candle">Candle<span>7th century BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=beekeeping">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
